from pathlib import Path
import json,html
p=Path('outputs/research_questions_20260915');base='https://github.com/ndandnd/EVSP-DR/blob/codex/parallel-research-20260911/outputs/'
parts=[]
def para(t):parts.append('<p>'+t+'</p>')
def h(t):parts.append('<h2>'+t+'</h2>')
def table(headers,rows):parts.append('<table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse"><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+html.escape(str(v))+'</th>' for v in headers)+'</tr>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(str(v))+'</td>' for v in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</table>')
parts.append('<h1>EVSP–DR: the research questions</h1>');para('Evidence verified 15 September, 10:24 EDT. This page contains conclusions and open questions; detailed experiment logs belong in the linked sources.')
h('1a. Can sequential CG + MIP recover GIRO’s fleet?')
para('<b>Yes under the baseline model: all six chains reach target 25; three reach 28.</b> Each step adds another GIRO duty’s trips and imports earlier generated columns. These are constructed smaller problems, so this is a useful strategy that uses the known duty grouping—not a solve from an unstructured trip set.')
table(['Largest target matched','C1','C2','C3','C4','C5','C6'],[['Original one-hour MIP',26,28,27,27,26,28],['Including separate longer MIPs',26,28,28,27,26,28]])
para('These are largest observed matches, not guaranteed cutoffs. Tests through 32 are launched. Matching k buses establishes a feasible fleet; a proof using saved columns does not establish the full model’s optimum. Baseline: 240 kWh/240 kW, covering, no reserve, shared charger capacity or ending-SOC floor.')
h('1b. Does a fresh start catch up with the accumulated time?')
para('<b>No in the completed comparison: warm 24/24, fresh 6/24.</b> Fresh CG starts with single-trip routes and receives T = the sum of earlier import + CG times through k. Both pools get the same one-hour MIP allowance.')
table(['Target k','Warm matches','Fresh matches'],[[5,'6/6','5/6'],[8,'6/6','1/6'],[10,'6/6','0/6'],[15,'6/6','0/6']])
para('The largest tested fresh match is k=8, chain 6. All fresh CGs converge before T expires: more ordinary CG time alone would not change their stopping decision. Four fresh pools provably need more than k buses; 14 other misses remain open. This is retrospective: historical code/hardware differ. T excludes intermediate MIPs; graph construction is accounted for separately. There is no mathematical guarantee that either heuristic must dominate.')
h('2. Which algorithm changes worked?')
para('<b>Summer:</b> event-based time/SOC graphs allow more charging choices, and exact shortest-path pricing can certify that no improving route remains in the modeled graph. This is stronger than the older capped label search; it is not a controlled historical speedup claim.')
para('<b>Recent controlled tests:</b> indexed route replay reduced CG time 12.4–18.1%; skipping unused LP-matrix construction saved 9.3–14.6%. Keeping all inherited routes rather than 512 cut CG time 40.6–64.5% and improved the three tested fleets from 9→8, 11→10 and 17→15. These are selected-input results; percentages cannot be added. Capacity-pricing caching has not demonstrated a speedup.')
h('3. Without the start fee, does changing duties beat charging optimization alone?')
para('<b>Not demonstrated.</b> On one five-duty set, optimized fixed GIRO duties and the joint saved-pool solution tie at all three tariff peaks: 128.29 (08:00), 164.23 (12:00), and 95.29 (18:00), in tariff cost units. Both use five buses and meet the same aggregate ending-energy requirement. Original GIRO charging repriced under those tariffs costs about 230–231, 290–291 and 223–224.')
para('Crucially, the joint test reprices saved CG columns and adds optimized fixed-duty alternatives; it does not rerun the full CG loop under zero fee and the new ending-energy constraint. It uses 350 kW, not the chain baseline’s 240 kW. Therefore GIRO’s duties are competitive in this tested pool—not proved globally optimal. <b>Next decisive test:</b> matched-physics, zero-fee CG + MIP versus optimized fixed duties, with equal fleet and ending-energy requirements.')
h('Two additional questions that matter')
para('<b>4. Why do we miss a target?</b> Separate unfinished pricing, missing useful integer routes, unfinished MIP search and model infeasibility. We already have evidence for both missing columns and insufficient integer search; each miss needs its own diagnosis.')
para('<b>5. Do the conclusions survive realistic charging constraints?</b> Reserve, station power/capacity and ending SOC remain incompletely tested together. Keep those results separate from baseline scaling. Decomposition is a possible method for scaling; broader instances and tariffs test whether the findings generalize.')
para('<b>Priorities:</b> finish chain reach and target-gap diagnoses; complete the fair zero-fee comparison. Keep detailed pool variants and scheduler counts off this page.')
para('<a href="'+base+'overnight_next_20260914/status_20260915T141509Z/README.md">Chain evidence and diagnostics</a> · <a href="'+base+'cumulative_budget_20260913/README.md">Accumulated-time experiment</a> · <a href="'+base+'controlled_comparison_20260913/status_20260913T063519Z/README.md">Algorithm comparisons</a> · <a href="'+base+'research_questions_20260915/evidence.json">Charging comparison evidence</a>')
para('<a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hDSWYb2KG-8pnLFN9BdSKkbXOjhytm4bxQz_MsBw_BY/edit?tab=t.ts4vwph3s99i">Figures with explanations</a> · <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hDSWYb2KG-8pnLFN9BdSKkbXOjhytm4bxQz_MsBw_BY/edit?tab=t.h5h2ivyiprly">CG curves and bus schedules</a> · <a href="'+base+'research_questions_20260915/doc_before.md">Previous detailed dashboard</a>')
